  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The South Sudanese Workers and Trade Unions Federation (SSWTUF) organized new members and extended social dialogue and collective bargaining protection coverage. The membership increased from 82,549 in December 2019 to 118,100 in May 2021. This is an increase of 29.63 per cent, with the majority of new members coming from the informal economy.

Betweeen September 2020  and March 2021 SSWTUF representatives influenced tripartite consultations on wages and social security leading to the adoption of a new wage structure and social insurance policy for public sector workers.  The policy is now approved by Cabinet and awaiting enactment by the Legislative Assembly for implementation. This development spurred workers to agitate for social protection coverage. The South Sudanese government is exploring various social protection floor options aimed at extending social protection coverage to people in the informal economy.

In September 2020, SSWTUF developed a roadmap to underpin its actions to prevent and mitigate the impact of COVID-19 in the world of work, including measures for the safe return to work after lockdowns. The federation also developed and presented for negotiation advocacy papers on the eradication of child labour and the fight against the chronic problem of the youth employment crisis in South Sudan. ILO supported the SSWTUF in developing an organizing resource manual which will continue to guide the SSWTUF and its affiliated unions in their campaigns for recruiting new members.

The ILO assisted the SSWTUF in identifying obstacles to extending social protection to people in the informal economy and drew the Federation&apos;s attention to the use of ILO Recommendations Nos. 202 and 204 as effective tools while exploring two policy approaches, namely extending coverage through tax-funded schemes and expanding social insurance coverage and other contributory mechanisms to achieve this goal. 

SSWTUF&apos;s work on the advocacy papers on the eradication of child labour and the fight against the chronic problem of the youth employment crisis in South Sudan were developed within the frameworks of the ILO&apos;s flagship programme on Jobs for ILO Peace and Resilience and Recommendation No. 204.

In addition, through technical advice, the ILO assisted the SSWTUF in identifying key messages to inform their environmental protection campaigns.</narrative>
   </description>
